module std.methods

// WHAT A METHOD'S RESULT DENOTES, carried here because the corpus already depends on the answer and
// no authority owned it. The finding that forced this row (2026-09-03): first() is realized four ways
// -- the v1 interpreter, and the rust/python/typescript emission templates -- and on a NON-EMPTY list
// whose element type is itself optional the four do not agree. Three collapse "the collection had no
// first element" and "the first element is itself absent" into one sentinel; the rust realization
// keeps them apart. Nothing declared which was correct, so each realization improvised.
//
// It is not an open question, and that is why this is a codification rather than a design decision:
// accepted source on main already fixes the denotation by RELYING on it. std.target_representation
// representation_spelling_for is declared `-> RepresentationSpelling<R>?` and returns `|> first`
// directly as that optional; resolve_exact_binding matches `hits |> first` against Present/Absent;
// checkpoint_row_migration_for does the same. The fact was load-bearing and UNHOMED -- DESIGN section
// 3's missing-home case -- not undecided.
type MethodResultDenotation
  = ElementAccessOptional
  | MethodResultDenotationUndeclared

type MethodDeclaration {
  name: String
  result_denotation: MethodResultDenotation
}

data add_method: MethodDeclaration = { name: "add", result_denotation: MethodResultDenotationUndeclared }
data all_method: MethodDeclaration = { name: "all", result_denotation: MethodResultDenotationUndeclared }
data any_method: MethodDeclaration = { name: "any", result_denotation: MethodResultDenotationUndeclared }
